# Intent: Fast eyes, part 2. The face invalidates only what changed: shape lists of the eyes, the brows and the mouth are matched shape by shape, an unchanged shape adds nothing, a changed one adds the bounds of its old and new place, and the pieces are merged to at most two areas per eye, one per brow and two for the mouth (LVGL falls back to a full redraw when its 32 invalid areas run out, and every area costs a pass over the object tree). A colour step of the emotion tint is redrawn only when the 5-6-5 value the screen can show changes, and only over the shapes, not over the whole art object (it was a full screen redraw on every step of every tint change: 76.8 thousand pixels per frame on the host model, 27 to 37 thousand now). A first version that sent thin strips along the outlines made a refresh slower on the unit (more areas, 8 ms of CPU per pass) and was dropped. One scratch set of rectangles is shared by all parts (the face task has a 4 KB stack: a set on its stack overflowed and reset the unit).
# Placement: unit (the face runs on the robot, so it is complete with no host)
# Upstream: firmware/main/stackchan/avatar/skins/default/{eyes.cpp,mouth.cpp,default.cpp} of the StackChan main component (MIT) as changed by X01, V10, V12; new file hal/maipai_dirty_core.h
# Check after a flash: Big eyes drift, blink, change emotion and speak with no stale pixel or ghost edge (the host check: hostrender `verify` compares the screen the unit would hold with a fresh full draw on every frame of a scripted run); host/tests/firmware/dirty_core_test.c passes; face_task_stack_min_free in self.face.bench_run stays above 300.

